-    "content": "# Scripted Workflows and Transition Helpers\n\n## Overview\n\nIn traditional project management tools, workflow rules are either simple notification triggers or inflexible webhooks that fire only *after* a change has occurred. If an issue is improperly moved to \"Done\" without meeting code coverage requirements or QA verification, the damage is already done.\n\n\ud83d\udc4dkanban implements a **Scripted Workflow Engine** with **Pre-Transition Helpers**. When an operator or agent attempts to move a work item between states (`from_state -> to_state`), the transition is evaluated through an ordered pipeline of synchronous validators, field mutators, and asynchronous AI agent gates.\n\n---\n\n## The Transition Helper Pipeline\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    Drag[\"User / Agent moves card: In Progress -> Done\"] --> HelperEngine[\"Transition Helper Engine\"]\n    HelperEngine --> Stage1[\"Stage 1: Synchronous Validator (Tier A Wasm)<br/>- Verify blockers resolved<br/>- Verify Definition of Done criteria & PR links\"]\n    Stage1 -->|PASS| Stage2[\"Stage 2: Synchronous Field Mutator (Tier A)<br/>- Auto-set closed_at timestamp<br/>- Update cycle velocity counters\"]\n    Stage1 -->|VETO| VetoInstant[\"Transition Rejected (422 Unprocessable)\"]\n    Stage2 --> Stage3[\"Stage 3: Asynchronous Gate Hold (Tier B Async Agent)<br/>- Card enters pending_agent_gate hold (\u23f3\ud83e\udd16)<br/>- Dispatches forensic audit to agent\"]\n    Stage3 -->|Approved| Approved[\"Gate Approved (\u2705\ud83e\udd16)<br/>Card lands cleanly in Done column\"]\n    Stage3 -->|Vetoed| VetoSnap[\"Gate Veto (\ud83d\uded1\ud83e\udd16)<br/>240ms spring snap-back to In Progress<br/>Itemized rejection posted to activity feed\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n## Helper Architecture\n\nTransition helpers operate across two execution tiers to balance latency and verification depth:\n\n1. **Tier A (Synchronous Wasm / In-Process):** Fast sub-25ms validators and mutators executing in memory or within sandboxed WebAssembly runtimes. These guarantee instantaneous rejection or field stamping without network round-trips.\n2. **Tier B (Asynchronous Autonomous Agent Gates):** Non-blocking background gates dispatched to local CLI harnesses (`agy -p`, `codex -p`), in-app SDK runners, or remote bare Git mirrors. These run comprehensive multi-file audits, build verifications, and forensic QA checks before approving the transition.\n\n### Failure Policies\n\nEvery attached transition helper specifies an explicit failure policy to determine behavior when a helper times out, errors, or fails execution:\n\n- **Fail-Closed:** Blocks the transition immediately. The card snaps back to its origin column and an itemized failure explanation is appended to the ticket timeline.\n- **Fail-Open (Warn):** Logs an audit warning to the activity feed and ledger while allowing the transition to proceed. This is reserved for non-critical informational checks.\n\n---\n\n## Admin Workflow Transition Studio\n\nProject Administrators configure transition pipelines through the visual studio at `/projects/KAN/settings/workflows`.\n\n![Workflow Transition Studio and Dry-Run Drawer](images/15-workflow-transition-studio.png)\n\n### Studio Capabilities\n- **State Transition Matrix:** Select any valid transition path (e.g. `In Progress -> In Review`, `In Review -> Done`).\n- **Pipeline Reordering:** Drag to reorder validators and mutators in the execution chain.\n- **Mutable Field Checkboxes:** Explicitly define which fields the transition helper is permitted to modify, enforcing least-privilege mutation boundaries.\n\n### The Non-Persistent Dry-Run Drawer\nTesting workflow logic against live tickets risks corrupting sprint metrics. The Workflow Studio provides a **Zero-Persistence Dry-Run Drawer**:\n- Select an existing ticket fixture or pick a live issue.\n- Execute the transition pipeline in a simulated sandbox without mutating the database.\n- Inspect per-step execution timings (e.g. `1.2ms` Wasm validator, `3.4ms` security invariant validator, `2.1ms` auto-label mutator).\n- Preview proposed property mutations (such as automatically applied labels).\n- Copy the exact CLI validation command (`kanban-agent validate-transition`) for local offline debugging.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    actor Admin as Workflow Architect\n    participant Studio as Workflow Studio\n    participant Drawer as Dry-Run Drawer\n    participant Engine as Helper Engine (Sandbox)\n\n    Admin->>Studio: Select \"In Review -> Done\" rule\n    Admin->>Studio: Click \"Dry-Run Against Sample Ticket\"\n    Studio->>Drawer: Open Dry-Run Drawer with sample payload\n    Admin->>Drawer: Click \"Run Dry-Run\"\n    Drawer->>Engine: Dispatch pipeline in dry_run=True mode\n    Engine->>Engine: Run Hook 1: qa-proof-validator (1.2ms, Allowed)\n    Engine->>Engine: Run Hook 2: security-invariant-validator (3.4ms, Allowed)\n    Engine->>Engine: Run Hook 3: auto-label-mutator (2.1ms, Applied labels)\n    Engine-->>Drawer: Return execution report & copyable CLI snippet\n    Drawer-->>Admin: Display green \"Transition Allowed: Changes would commit\"\n```\n\n![Workflow Studio Dry-Run Drawer Simulation](images/50-workflow-studio-dry-run-drawer.png)\n\n---\n\n## Read-Only Bare Git Repository Bindings\n\nAutonomous agents evaluating transition gates often require access to source code, diff trees, and commit histories. To satisfy this requirement securely without risking repository corruption:\n- Administrators can bind local bare Git mirrors directly to a project through the **Git Repository Bindings** dialog.\n- The volume mount is strictly enforced as read-only (`:ro bare mirror`).\n- Agents can run `git diff`, `git log`, and code analysis against the mirror, but all write attempts fail at the OS kernel boundary.\n\n![Git Repository Bindings Modal](images/51-workflow-bind-git-repo-modal.png)\n\n---\n\n## Asynchronous Agent Gate Lifecycle on the Board\n\nWhen an issue transition requires complex verification (such as forensic QA certification or automated security audits), the transition operates asynchronously without blocking the user interface.\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> InProgress\n    InProgress --> PendingGate: Move to Done (Trigger Transition Helper)\n    PendingGate --> Done: Agent Certifies Criteria (\u2705\ud83e\udd16 Approved)\n    PendingGate --> InProgress: Agent Rejects (\ud83d\uded1\ud83e\udd16 240ms Snap-Back)\n    Done --> [*]\n```\n\n![Async Agent Gate Card States and Snap-Back Lifecycle](images/16-async-agent-gate-states.png)\n\n![Board Pending Gate State in Active Sprint](images/35-board-pending-gate.png)\n\n### The Board Interaction States\n\n1. **Pending Hold (`\u23f3\ud83e\udd16`):**\n   - The card displays an amber countdown timer and progress indicator.\n   - The ticket enters database status `pending_agent_gate`.\n   - Other users viewing the board see the real-time hold status over WebSockets.\n2. **Gate Veto and 240ms Spring Snap-Back (`\ud83d\uded1\ud83e\udd16`):**\n   - If the agent discovers a defect or missing verification proof, the gate is vetoed.\n   - The board executes a physics-based 240ms spring snap-back animation, returning the card to its original column.\n   - A non-intrusive toast notification alerts the user, and the agent automatically posts an itemized rejection explanation to the ticket's `\ud83e\udd16 Gates` activity feed.\n3. **Gate Approval (`\u2705\ud83e\udd16`):**\n   - When all criteria are certified, the gate resolves to approved.\n   - The card transitions smoothly into the destination column, stamped with an immutable cryptographic verification badge.\n"
